{"causes":["Jimmy Wales and Larry Sanger's vision for a free and open internet.","The growing demand for accessible and reliable information in the digital age.","The need for collaborative content creation and open-source innovation."],"effects":["Democratization of access to knowledge.","Promoting digital literacy and critical thinking skills among underrepresented communities.","Inspiring new models for collaborative content creation and open-source innovation."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Jimmy Wales","sourceName":"Jimmy Wales","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"wikimedia-foundation","targetName":"Wikimedia Foundation","context":"In 2003, Jimmy Wales founded the Wikimedia Foundation as a non-profit organization."},{"sourceSlug":"Larry Sanger","sourceName":"Larry Sanger","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"wikimedia-foundation","targetName":"Wikimedia Foundation","context":"Larry Sanger joined Jimmy Wales in 2003 to co-found the Wikimedia Foundation."},{"sourceSlug":"Wikipedia","sourceName":"Wikipedia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"wikimedia-foundation","targetName":"Wikimedia Foundation","context":"The Wikimedia Foundation supports and promotes the creation, editing, and maintenance of Wikipedia."},{"sourceSlug":"Google","sourceName":"Google","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"wikimedia-foundation","targetName":"Wikimedia Foundation","context":"In 2019, Google partnered with the Wikimedia Foundation to improve digital literacy and critical thinking skills among underrepresented communities."},{"sourceSlug":"UNESCO","sourceName":"UNESCO","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"wikimedia-foundation","targetName":"Wikimedia Foundation","context":"In 2018, UNESCO recognized the Wikimedia Foundation's efforts to promote digital literacy and critical thinking skills among underrepresented communities."},{"sourceSlug":"Wikipedia editors","sourceName":"Wikipedia editors","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"wikimedia-foundation","targetName":"Wikimedia Foundation","context":"Millions of Wikipedia editors contribute to the platform every month."},{"sourceSlug":"Google trends","sourceName":"Google trends","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"wikimedia-foundation","targetName":"Wikimedia Foundation","context":"The Wikimedia Foundation's impact can be seen in Google Trends, which tracks the popularity of Wikipedia and other related topics."},{"sourceSlug":"Wikipedia views","sourceName":"Wikipedia views","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"wikimedia-foundation","targetName":"Wikimedia Foundation","context":"Over 50 million registered users access Wikipedia every month, with millions more viewing the platform daily."}],"places":[{"name":"San Francisco, California","role":"Headquarters"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Wikimedia Foundation","type":"Reference article","slug":"wikimedia-foundation"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q180","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikimedia_Foundation"],"tags":["United States","charitable organization","Americas","Humanitarian & Relief Organizations","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Wikimedia Foundation is remembered as a landmark institution of the Contemporary era, referenced in 210 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Wikimedia Foundation is an American non-profit organisation founded on 20 June 2003 by Wikipedia co-founder Jimmy Wales — established to host and support the free knowledge projects including Wikipedia (launched 2001), Wikimedia Commons, Wikidata, Wikisource, Wiktionary, and related sister projects. Headquartered in San Francisco, California, the Wikimedia Foundation is one of the most significant organisations in the history of the internet and of human knowledge production: Wikipedia, its primary project, has become the world's largest and most widely consulted encyclopaedia — with over 60 million articles in approximately 330 languages as of 2024, serving approximately 1.5 billion unique devices per month.

The foundation's mission is to make the sum of all human knowledge freely available to every person on the planet — a goal stated in explicitly universalist terms that has positioned the Wikimedia projects as a global public infrastructure for knowledge access. Unlike the major internet platforms (Google, Facebook, Amazon), the Wikimedia Foundation is a non-profit that operates without advertising revenue, depending entirely on donations from millions of individuals and institutional grants. This financial model — which has been periodically controversial as Wikipedia's servers and staff costs have grown — has preserved the encyclopaedia's independence from commercial pressures and advertising influence.

Wikipedia's epistemological impact on human knowledge access has been transformative: it is the first reference source consulted by hundreds of millions of people globally, it has displaced commercial encyclopaedias (Encyclopædia Britannica ceased print publication in 2012), and its collaborative authoring model — in which millions of volunteer editors create, verify, and maintain content under transparent policies — represents an entirely new model of knowledge production. Wikidata (launched 2012) has become a major linked data infrastructure for the semantic web, while Wikimedia Commons holds over 90 million freely licensed media files that are used across the internet.","subjects":["Digital History","Internet History","Contemporary Era","Knowledge Access","Information Technology","Open Source","Encyclopedia","Non-profit Organisations"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","WORLD_SYSTEMS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Wikimedia Foundation's impact extends beyond its own boundaries, shaping the way we interact with knowledge and each other. Its legacy will continue to be felt for generations to come.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
